1e:["$","$1a","0",{"children":[["$","$1a","0",{"children":[["$","p","0",{"children":["Write the format as a contract with fixed and variable parts. Keep the opening situation, proof device or closing question stable where that helps comprehension. Change the actual problem and evidence. During review, ask whether the new episode teaches something the previous one did not. Maintain an archive of attempted variations so repetition is intentional rather than accidental. A formatted account can still experiment; it simply changes one part of a recognisable structure at a time. The format is valuable when it makes the next useful idea easier to understand, not when it fills a calendar without decisions."]}]]}]]}]
1f:["$","$1a","1",{"children":[["$","$1a","0",{"children":[["$","p","0",{"children":["A recurring format gives a collaboration somewhere to land. In the Khaby reference, the cited interview and caption identify a guest and an apple premise; the account's established identity is the context for that collaboration. The broader lesson is to decide which part of your series remains stable when a new guest, product or problem appears. It might be the question, the reveal or the way a common complication is examined. Write a version with an unfamiliar subject to test the structure. If the format only works with one exceptional guest, it may be a successful episode rather than a sustainable programme."]}]]}]]}]
20:["$","blockquote",null,{"children":["“","A recurring format beats an empty template.","”"]}]
